        "detail": "Created HowToPlayModal component (app/components/HowToPlayModal.tsx) and exported it from the components barrel (app/components/index.ts).\n\n## Implementation Details\n\n**Component structure:**\n- `HowToPlayModal({ onClose })` — main modal component\n- `TileIcon({ type })` — inline SVG tile icons for straight/curve descriptions\n- `CloverRow({ count, label })` — reuses the clover SVG pattern from LevelComplete\n\n**Three content sections (per acceptance criteria):**\n1. **Tile Placement** — explains straight vs curve tiles with inline SVG icons, how to select and place\n2. **Rotation & Editing** — rotate via click, remove via right-click or remove tool, drag to move\n3. **Scoring & Clovers** — Send Lucky button, par system, clover rating table (3/2/1 clovers), retry\n\n**Accessibility:**\n- `role=\"dialog\"` + `aria-modal=\"true\"` + `aria-label`\n- Focus trapped inside modal via Tab/Shift+Tab keydown handler\n- Focus auto-moves to close button on mount\n- Focus returns to previous element on unmount (useRef captures document.activeElement)\n- Escape key dismisses modal\n- Backdrop click dismisses modal\n- Close button has `aria-label=\"Close\"`\n- Decorative SVGs have `aria-hidden=\"true\"`\n\n**Visual consistency:**\n- Same overlay pattern as LevelComplete: `fixed inset-0 bg-black/30 backdrop-blur-sm z-50 animate-fadeIn`\n- Same card pattern: `bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-2xl animate-scaleIn`\n- Same button styles: secondary emerald button for \"Got it!\"\n- Same color palette: emerald-900/800/700, gray-600/400, amber-600\n- Same clover SVG rendering from LevelComplete\n- Scrollable content area with `max-h-[85dvh]` for mobile\n\n**Decisions:**\n- No component render tests added — project's vitest is configured for `.test.ts` only (no jsdom/happy-dom environment). Existing test suite covers pure functions only. The sibling keyboard shortcut task will exercise integration.\n- Used inline sub-components (TileIcon, CloverRow) rather than separate files since they're modal-specific presentation details, consistent with how LevelComplete keeps everything in one file.\n- Did NOT import TilePreview from the components barrel — that component depends on engine imports (getConnection, getEdgePoint) and uses a more complex API. The inline TileIcon is simpler and purpose-built for the help context."
